Task 1: Install OpenShift Virtualization Operator via Web Console
Subtask 1.1: Access the OpenShift Web Console
Open your web browser and navigate to the OpenShift Web Console URL provided in your lab environment

Log in using the credentials provided:

Username: [provided in lab environment]
Password: [provided in lab environment]
Verify you have cluster-admin access by checking the top-right corner shows your username with administrative privileges

Subtask 1.2: Navigate to OperatorHub
In the left navigation menu, click on Operators

Select OperatorHub from the dropdown menu

You will see the OperatorHub catalog with various operators available for installation

Subtask 1.3: Search and Select OpenShift Virtualization Operator
In the search box at the top of the OperatorHub page, type:

OpenShift Virtualization
Click on the OpenShift Virtualization operator tile (it should show Red Hat as the provider)

Review the operator details including:

Description
Supported OpenShift versions
Capabilities
Required permissions
Subtask 1.4: Install the Operator
Click the Install button

On the installation page, configure the following settings:

Update Channel: Select stable (recommended for production)
Installation Mode: Choose All namespaces on the cluster
Installed Namespace: Select openshift-cnv (this will be created automatically)
Update Approval: Select Automatic for this lab
Click Install to begin the installation process

Wait for the installation to complete. You should see a green checkmark and "Succeeded" status

Subtask 1.5: Verify Installation via Web Console
Navigate to Operators > Installed Operators

Ensure you're viewing All Projects or specifically the openshift-cnv project

Verify that OpenShift Virtualization appears in the list with status Succeeded

Click on the operator name to view its details and available APIs

Task 2: Install OpenShift Virtualization Operator via CLI
Note: If you completed Task 1, you can skip this task or uninstall the operator first to practice CLI installation.

Subtask 2.1: Prepare CLI Environment
Open a terminal in your lab environment

Verify OpenShift CLI is installed and you're logged in:

oc version
oc whoami
Check your current context and ensure you have cluster-admin privileges:

oc auth can-i create operators --all-namespaces
Subtask 2.2: Create Namespace for OpenShift Virtualization
Create the required namespace:

oc create namespace openshift-cnv
Verify the namespace was created:

oc get namespaces | grep openshift-cnv
Subtask 2.3: Create OperatorGroup
Create an OperatorGroup YAML file:

cat > cnv-operatorgroup.yaml << EOF
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1
kind: OperatorGroup
metadata:
  name: kubevirt-hyperconverged-group
  namespace: openshift-cnv
spec:
  targetNamespaces:
  - openshift-cnv
EOF
Apply the OperatorGroup:

oc apply -f cnv-operatorgroup.yaml
Verify the OperatorGroup was created:

oc get operatorgroup -n openshift-cnv
Subtask 2.4: Create Subscription
Create a Subscription YAML file:

cat > cnv-subscription.yaml << EOF
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: hco-operatorhub
  namespace: openshift-cnv
spec:
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
  name: kubevirt-hyperconverged
  channel: stable
EOF
Apply the Subscription:

oc apply -f cnv-subscription.yaml
Verify the subscription was created:

oc get subscription -n openshift-cnv
Subtask 2.5: Monitor Installation Progress
Watch the ClusterServiceVersion (CSV) installation:

oc get csv -n openshift-cnv -w
Wait until the CSV shows Succeeded phase (this may take several minutes)

Check the operator pod status:

oc get pods -n openshift-cnv
Task 3: Verify Installation by Checking KubeVirt CRDs and Resources
Subtask 3.1: Check Custom Resource Definitions
List all KubeVirt-related CRDs:

oc get crd | grep kubevirt
Look for these specific CRDs:

oc get crd | grep -E "(virtualmachines|virtualmachineinstances|hyperconvergeds)"
Get detailed information about the VirtualMachine CRD:

oc describe crd virtualmachines.kubevirt.io
Subtask 3.2: Verify HyperConverged Custom Resource
Check if HyperConverged CR exists:

oc get hyperconverged -n openshift-cnv
If it doesn't exist, create it:

cat > hyperconverged-cr.yaml << EOF
apiVersion: hco.kubevirt.io/v1beta1
kind: HyperConverged
metadata:
  name: kubevirt-hyperconverged
  namespace: openshift-cnv
spec: {}
EOF
Apply the HyperConverged CR:

oc apply -f hyperconverged-cr.yaml
Monitor the HyperConverged deployment:

oc get hyperconverged kubevirt-hyperconverged -n openshift-cnv -o yaml
Subtask 3.3: Verify KubeVirt Components
Check KubeVirt deployment status:

oc get kubevirt -n openshift-cnv
Verify all KubeVirt pods are running:

oc get pods -n openshift-cnv
Check for these essential components:

virt-api
virt-controller
virt-handler (DaemonSet)
virt-operator
Verify the virt-handler DaemonSet is running on all nodes:

oc get daemonset -n openshift-cnv
Subtask 3.4: Test API Accessibility
Test if you can access VirtualMachine resources:

oc get virtualmachines --all-namespaces
Test VirtualMachineInstance access:

oc get virtualmachineinstances --all-namespaces
Check available API resources:

oc api-resources | grep kubevirt
Task 4: Final Verification and Health Check
Subtask 4.1: Comprehensive Status Check
Create a comprehensive status check script:

cat > verify-cnv-installation.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== OpenShift Virtualization Installation Verification ==="
echo

echo "1. Checking namespace..."
oc get namespace openshift-cnv
echo

echo "2. Checking operator installation..."
oc get csv -n openshift-cnv
echo

echo "3. Checking HyperConverged status..."
oc get hyperconverged -n openshift-cnv
echo

echo "4. Checking KubeVirt status..."
oc get kubevirt -n openshift-cnv
echo

echo "5. Checking all pods status..."
oc get pods -n openshift-cnv
echo

echo "6. Checking CRDs..."
oc get crd | grep kubevirt | wc -l
echo "KubeVirt CRDs found"
echo

echo "7. Checking node readiness for virtualization..."
oc get nodes -o wide
echo

echo "=== Verification Complete ==="
EOF
Make the script executable and run it:

chmod +x verify-cnv-installation.sh
./verify-cnv-installation.sh
Subtask 4.2: Check Feature Gates and Capabilities
Verify that virtualization features are enabled:

oc get hyperconverged kubevirt-hyperconverged -n openshift-cnv -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}' | jq .
Check if the cluster supports virtualization:

oc describe nodes | grep -i "kvm\|virtualization"
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Operator Installation Stuck
Symptoms: CSV remains in "Installing" phase for extended time

Solution:

# Check operator logs
oc logs -n openshift-cnv deployment/hyperconverged-cluster-operator

# Check for resource constraints
oc describe nodes | grep -A 5 "Allocated resources"
Issue 2: Pods in CrashLoopBackOff
Symptoms: KubeVirt pods failing to start

Solution:

# Check pod logs
oc logs -n openshift-cnv <pod-name>

# Check node compatibility
oc get nodes -o yaml | grep -i "feature\|cpu"
Issue 3: Missing CRDs
Symptoms: VirtualMachine CRDs not found

Solution:

# Restart the operator
oc delete pod -n openshift-cnv -l name=hyperconverged-cluster-operator

# Check operator subscription
oc get subscription -n openshift-cnv -o yaml
